The '''1982 AFA World Cup''' was the eighth edition of the [[AFA World Cup]], the quadrennial international men's [[association football]] championship contested by the senior national teams of the member associations of the '''[[Adonian Football Association]]''' (AFA). It was held in [[Okaiken]] from 12 June to 10 July 1982. It was the first AFA World Cup hosted in East Fosia.

The tournament featured 36 teams and was contested across 12 venues in 12 cities. The format consisted of two group stages followed by a knockout round, with the final held at the newly constructed '''Tenji Park Olympic Stadium''' in [[Okami]].

[[Balisca national football team|Balisca]] won the tournament, defeating [[Asikyira national football team|Asikyira]] 3–1 in the final to claim their second AFA World Cup title. [[Sanrobonia national football team|Sanrobonia]] finished third by defeating hosts [[Okaiken national football team|Okaiken]] 2–0 in the third-place match. Balisca's [[Matías Varela]] was the tournament's top scorer with eight goals and was awarded the Golden Ball as the tournament's best player.

The tournament was a landmark event for Okaiken, occurring at the dawn of the '''[[Gogo 8090]]''' economic boom and showcasing the nation's transformation following the [[1968 Quiet Revolution]]. It was widely regarded as one of the most successful World Cups in AFA history, with record attendance and global television viewership.

==Host selection==
{{main|AFA World Cup hosts}}

Okaiken was awarded the hosting rights on 15 May 1978 at the AFA Congress in [[PLACEHOLDER]], defeating bids from [[Nakamizu]] and [[Dagit]].

The bid was championed by Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], who positioned the tournament as a showcase for Okaiken's modernization under the Republican government. Nakasone's successor, [[Junichiro Okazaki]], oversaw the initial preparations, while [[Naoto Emi]], who took office in January 1982, presided over the tournament itself.

==Venues==
Twelve stadiums in twelve host cities were selected for the tournament. Only one new stadium, '''Tenji Park Olympic Stadium''' in Okami, was constructed specifically for the World Cup, with a capacity of 80,000. The remaining eleven venues underwent extensive renovation to meet AFA requirements, with all stadiums expanded to capacities exceeding 30,000.

The selection of host cities reflected Okaiken's diverse geography and economic development, spanning the three main islands of [[Harujima]], [[Ishijima]], and [[Yagikoshu]].

